BT /F1 10 Tf 13 TL 50 800 Td (Raspberry Pi hardware documentation \(selected chapters\)) Tj T* () Tj T* (These measurements are based on a standard Raspberry Pi OS image \(current as of 26 February) Tj T* (2016, or June 2019 for Raspberry Pi 4\), at room temperature, with the Raspberry Pi connected to) Tj T* (an HDMI monitor, USB keyboard, and USB mouse. Raspberry Pi 3 Model B was connected to a wireless) Tj T* (LAN access point, and Raspberry Pi 4 was connected to Ethernet. All power measurements are) Tj T* (approximate and don't include power drawn by additional USB devices; total power consumption can) Tj T* (exceed these figures if additional USB devices or a HAT are attached.) Tj T* () Tj T* ([.whitepaper, title="Extra PMIC features on Raspberry Pi 4, Raspberry Pi 5 and Compute Module) Tj T* (4", subtitle="", link=https://pip.raspberrypi.com/documents/RP-004340-WP] A range of PMIC) Tj T* (devices have been used on Raspberry Pi 4, Raspberry Pi 5, and CM4. All the PMICs provide extra) Tj T* (functionality alongside that of voltage supply. This document describes how to access these) Tj T* (features in software.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Decrease Raspberry Pi 5 wattage when turned off) Tj T* () Tj T* (By default, the Raspberry Pi 5 consumes around 1 W to 1.4 W of power when turned off. This can) Tj T* (be decreased by manually editing the EEPROM configuration with sudo rpi-eeprom-config -e. Modify) Tj T* (the settings to add:) Tj T* () Tj T* (POWER_OFF_ON_HALT=1) Tj T* () Tj T* (This should drop the power consumption when powered down to around 0.01 W.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Power supply warnings) Tj T* () Tj T* (On all models of Raspberry Pi since the Raspberry Pi B+ \(2014\) except the Zero range, there is) Tj T* (low-voltage detection circuitry that will detect if the supply voltage drops below 4.63 V \(±5%\).) Tj T* (This will result in an entry being added to the kernel log.) Tj T* () Tj T* (If you see warnings, switch to a higher quality power supply and cable. Low quality power) Tj T* (supplies can corrupt storage or cause unpredictable behaviour within the Raspberry Pi.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Voltages can drop for a variety of reasons. You might have plugged in too many high-demand USB) Tj T* (devices. The power supply could be inadequate. Or the power supply cable could use wires that) Tj T* (are too thin.) Tj T* () Tj T* ([.whitepaper, title="Making a more resilient file system", subtitle="",) Tj T* (link=https://pip.raspberrypi.com/documents/RP-003610-WP] Raspberry Pi devices are frequently) Tj T* (used as data storage and monitoring devices, often in places where sudden power-downs can occur.) Tj T* (As with any computing device, power dropouts can cause storage corruption.) Tj T* () Tj T* (This white paper provides some options on how to prevent data corruption under these and other) Tj T* (circumstances by selecting appropriate file systems and setups to ensure data integrity.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Power supplies and Raspberry Pi OS) Tj T* () Tj T* (The bootloader passes information about the power supply through device-tree /proc/device-) Tj T* (tree/chosen/power. Users will typically not read this directly.) Tj T* () Tj T* (max_current:: The max current in mA uspd_power_data_objects:: A dump of the PDOs – debug for) Tj T* (advanced users usb_max_current_enable:: Whether the current limiter was set to high or low) Tj T* (usb_over_current_detected:: Whether any USB over current occurred during boot before) Tj T* () Tj T* (Page 3 of 16) Tj T* ET
endstream
