BT /F1 10 Tf 13 TL 50 800 Td (Raspberry Pi hardware documentation \(selected chapters\)) Tj T* () Tj T* (transferring control to the OS reset_event:: The PMIC reset reason, for example, watchdog, over-) Tj T* (or under-voltage, over-temperature) Tj T* () Tj T* (The PMIC has built-in ADCs that, among other things, can measure the supply voltage EXT5V_V. Use) Tj T* (the following command to view ADC measurements:) Tj T* () Tj T* ($ vcgencmd pmic_read_adc) Tj T* () Tj T* (You can't see USB current or anything else connected directly to 5V, because this bypasses the) Tj T* (PMIC. You should not expect this to add up to the wattage of the source power supply. However,) Tj T* (it can be useful to monitor things like the core voltage.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Back-powering) Tj T* () Tj T* (The USB specification requires that USB devices must not supply current to upstream devices. If) Tj T* (a USB device does supply current to an upstream device, then this is called back-powering. Often) Tj T* (this happens when a badly-made powered USB hub is connected, and will result in the powered USB) Tj T* (hub supplying power to the host Raspberry Pi. We don't recommended this because power supplied) Tj T* (to the Raspberry Pi through the hub bypasses the protection circuitry built into the Raspberry) Tj T* (Pi, leaving it vulnerable to damage in the event of a power surge.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Raspberry Pi revision codes) Tj T* () Tj T* (Each distinct Raspberry Pi model revision has a unique revision code. You can look up a) Tj T* (Raspberry Pi device's revision code by running:) Tj T* () Tj T* ($ cat /proc/cpuinfo) Tj T* () Tj T* (The last three lines show the hardware type, the revision code, and the Raspberry Pi device's) Tj T* (unique serial number. For example:) Tj T* () Tj T* (Hardware    : BCM2835 Revision    : a02082 Serial      : 00000000765fc593) Tj T* () Tj T* (NOTE: All Raspberry Pi computers report BCM2835, even those with BCM2836, BCM2837, BCM2711, and) Tj T* (BCM2712 processors. You should not use this string to detect the processor. Decode the revision) Tj T* (code using the information below, or cat /sys/firmware/devicetree/base/model. Depending on which) Tj T* (kernel you're running, your cpuinfo might also have a "Model" line, and might not have a) Tj T* ("Hardware" line.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Old-style revision codes) Tj T* () Tj T* (The first set of Raspberry Pi models were given sequential hex revision codes from 0002 to 0015:) Tj T* () Tj T* (Code | Model | Revision | RAM | Manufacturer) Tj T* (0002 | B | 1.0 | 256 MB | Egoman) Tj T* (0003 | B | 1.0 | 256 MB | Egoman) Tj T* (0004 | B | 2.0 | 256 MB | Sony UK) Tj T* (0005 | B | 2.0 | 256 MB | Qisda) Tj T* (0006 | B | 2.0 | 256 MB | Egoman) Tj T* (0007 | A | 2.0 | 256 MB | Egoman) Tj T* (0008 | A | 2.0 | 256 MB | Sony UK) Tj T* (0009 | A | 2.0 | 256 MB | Qisda) Tj T* () Tj T* (Page 4 of 16) Tj T* ET
