BT /F1 10 Tf 13 TL 50 800 Td (Raspberry Pi hardware documentation \(selected chapters\)) Tj T* () Tj T* (if new and model == 0x11 and mem >= 3 : # Note, 3 in the mem field is 2 GB print\("We are a 4B) Tj T* (with at least 2 GB RAM!"\)) Tj T* () Tj T* (Best practices for revision code usage) Tj T* () Tj T* (To avoid problems when new board revisions are created, do not use the revision code \(for) Tj T* (example, c03111\).) Tj T* () Tj T* (A naive implementation uses a list of supported revision codes, comparing the detected code with) Tj T* (the list to decide if the device is supported. This breaks when a new board revision comes out) Tj T* (or if the production location changes: each creates a new revision code not in the supported) Tj T* (revision code list. This would cause rejections of new revisions of the same board type, despite) Tj T* (the fact that they are always backwards-compatible. Every time a new revision appears, you would) Tj T* (have to release a new supported revision code list containing the new revision code – an onerous) Tj T* (support burden.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Instead, use one of the following approaches:) Tj T* () Tj T* (Filter by the board-type field \(3A, 4B, etc.\), which indicates the model, but not the revision.) Tj T* (Filter by the amount-of-memory field, since RAM vaguely corresponds to the computing power of a) Tj T* (board.) Tj T* () Tj T* (For instance, you could limit support to Raspberry Pi 4B models with 2 GB of RAM or more. The) Tj T* (examples in the previous section use this recommended approach.) Tj T* () Tj T* (NOTE: Always check bit 23, the 'New' flag, to ensure that the revision code is the new version) Tj T* (before checking any other fields.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Check Raspberry Pi model and CPU across distributions) Tj T* () Tj T* (Support and formatting for /proc/cpuinfo varies across Linux distributions. To check the model) Tj T* (or CPU of a Raspberry Pi device on any Linux distribution \(including Raspberry Pi OS\), check the) Tj T* (device tree:) Tj T* () Tj T* ($ cat /proc/device-tree/compatible | tr '\\0' '\\n') Tj T* (raspberrypi,5-model-b) Tj T* (brcm,bcm2712) Tj T* () Tj T* (This outputs two null-separated string values, each containing a comma-separated make and model.) Tj T* (For instance, the Raspberry Pi 5 outputs the board and CPU strings above. These correspond to) Tj T* (the following values:) Tj T* () Tj T* (raspberrypi \(board make\) 5-model-b \(board model\) brcm \(CPU make\) bcm2712 \(CPU model\)) Tj T* () Tj T* (Raspberry Pi models have the following device tree values:) Tj T* () Tj T* (Device Name | Make | Model | CPU Make | CPU) Tj T* (Raspberry Pi 500 /500+ | raspberrypi | 500 | brcm | bcm2712) Tj T* (Compute Module 5 | raspberrypi | 5-compute-module | brcm | bcm2712) Tj T* (Raspberry Pi 5 | raspberrypi | 5-model-b | brcm | bcm2712) Tj T* (Raspberry Pi 400 | raspberrypi | 400 | brcm | bcm2711) Tj T* (Compute Module 4S | raspberrypi | 4s-compute-module | brcm | bcm2711) Tj T* () Tj T* (Page 9 of 16) Tj T* ET
endstream
