BT /F1 10 Tf 13 TL 50 800 Td (Raspberry Pi hardware documentation \(selected chapters\)) Tj T* () Tj T* () Tj T* (109-114:: Factory device UUID Currently a 16-digit numerical id which should match the bar code) Tj T* (on the device. Padded with zero characters and c40 encoded.) Tj T* () Tj T* (This is available through device-tree in /proc/device-tree/chosen/rpi-duid.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Frequency management and thermal control) Tj T* () Tj T* (All Raspberry Pi models perform a degree of thermal management to avoid overheating under heavy) Tj T* (load. The SoCs have an internal temperature sensor, which software on the GPU polls to ensure) Tj T* (that temperatures do not exceed a limit which we define as 85°C on all models. It is possible to) Tj T* (set this to a lower value, but not to a higher one. As the device approaches the limit, various) Tj T* (frequencies and sometimes voltages used on the chip \(Arm, GPU\) are reduced. This reduces the) Tj T* (amount of heat generated, keeping the temperature under control.) Tj T* () Tj T* (When the core temperature is between 80°C and 85°C, the Arm cores will be progressively) Tj T* (throttled back. If the temperature reaches 85°C, both the Arm cores and the GPU will be) Tj T* (throttled back.) Tj T* () Tj T* (For Raspberry Pi 3 Model B+, the PCB technology has been changed to provide better heat) Tj T* (dissipation and increased thermal mass. In addition, a soft temperature limit has been) Tj T* (introduced, with the goal of maximising the time for which a device can "sprint" before reaching) Tj T* (the hard limit at 85°C. When the soft limit is reached, the clock speed is reduced from 1.4 GHz) Tj T* (to 1.2 GHz, and the operating voltage is reduced slightly. This reduces the rate of temperature) Tj T* (increase: we trade a short period at 1.4 GHz for a longer period at 1.2 GHz. By default, the) Tj T* (soft limit is 60°C, and this can be changed by using the temp_soft_limit setting in config.txt.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The Raspberry Pi 4 Model B continues with the same PCB technology as the Raspberry Pi 3 Model) Tj T* (B+, to help dissipate excess heat. There is currently no soft limit defined.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Use DVFS) Tj T* () Tj T* (NOTE: Discussion of DVFS applies to 4-series devices only \(Raspberry Pi 4B, Compute Module 4,) Tj T* (Compute Module 4S, and Raspberry Pi 400\).) Tj T* () Tj T* (Raspberry Pi 4 devices implement dynamic voltage and frequency scaling \(DVFS\). This technique) Tj T* (allows 4-series devices to run at lower temperatures whilst still providing the same) Tj T* (performance.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Various clocks \(for example, Arm, Core, V3D, ISP, H264, HEVC\) inside the SoC are monitored by) Tj T* (the firmware, and whenever they are not running at full speed, the voltage supplied to the) Tj T* (particular part of the chip driven by the clock is reduced relative to the reduction from full) Tj T* (speed. In effect, only enough voltage is supplied to keep the block running correctly at the) Tj T* (specific speed at which it is running. This can result in significant reductions in power used) Tj T* (by the SoC, and therefore in the overall heat being produced.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Due to possible system stability problems involved with running an undervoltage, especially when) Tj T* (using undervoltaged fixed clock peripherals \(eg. PCIe\), three DVFS modes are available and can) Tj T* (be configured in /boot/firmware/config.txt with the below properties. Most systems should use) Tj T* (dvfs=3, headless systems might benefit from a small power reduction with dvfs=1 at the risk of) Tj T* (PCIe stability issues.) Tj T* () Tj T* () Tj T* (Page 12 of 16) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
