BT /F1 10 Tf 13 TL 50 800 Td (Raspberry Pi hardware documentation \(selected chapters\)) Tj T* () Tj T* (property=value | Description) Tj T* (dvfs=1 | allow undervoltage) Tj T* (dvfs=2 | fixed voltage for default operating frequencies) Tj T* (dvfs=3 | scale voltage up on demand for over clocking \(default\). If over_voltage is specified in) Tj T* (  config.txt then dynamic voltage scaling is disabled causing the system to revert to dvfs=2.) Tj T* () Tj T* (NOTE: This setting has been removed on 5-series devices and is effectively always mode 3.) Tj T* () Tj T* (In addition, a more stepped CPU governor is also used to produce finer-grained control of Arm) Tj T* (core frequencies, which means the DVFS is more effective. The steps are now 1500 MHz, 1000 MHz,) Tj T* (750 MHz, and 600 MHz. These steps can also help when the SoC is being throttled, and mean that) Tj T* (throttling all the way back to 600 MHz is much less likely, giving an overall increase in fully) Tj T* (loaded performance.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The default CPU governor is ondemand. The governor can be manually changed with the cpufreq-set) Tj T* (command \(from the cpufrequtils package\) to reduce idle power consumption:) Tj T* () Tj T* ($ sudo apt install cpufrequtils $ sudo cpufreq-set -g powersave) Tj T* () Tj T* (Measure temperatures) Tj T* () Tj T* (Due to the architecture of the SoCs used on Raspberry Pi devices, and the use of the upstream) Tj T* (temperature monitoring code in the Raspberry Pi OS distribution, Linux-based temperature) Tj T* (measurements can be inaccurate. However, the vcgencmd command provides an accurate and) Tj T* (instantaneous reading of the current SoC temperature, as it communicates with the GPU directly:) Tj T* () Tj T* ($ vcgencmd measure_temp) Tj T* () Tj T* (Add heatsinks) Tj T* () Tj T* (Thanks to built-in throttling, heatsinks are not necessary to prevent overheating damage to the) Tj T* (SoC. However, a heatsink or small fan can reduce thermal throttling and improve performance.) Tj T* (Mount the Raspberry Pi vertically for the best airflow and thus slightly improved heat) Tj T* (dissipation.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Fan cases) Tj T* () Tj T* (To ensure the best performance for your Raspberry Pi, use an active cooling solution such as a) Tj T* (fan. Raspberry Pi firmware manages fan speeds for all official fans.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Raspberry Pi 4 fan) Tj T* () Tj T* (For Raspberry Pi 4, add the Raspberry Pi 4 Case Fan to the lid of the Raspberry Pi 4 case.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The fan is rated at 0.18 A, operates at 5 V DC supplied through the 40-pin GPIO header, and) Tj T* (typically draws 60 mA \(300 mW\).) Tj T* () Tj T* (Raspberry Pi 5 fans) Tj T* () Tj T* (For Raspberry Pi 5, use one of the official fan options:) Tj T* () Tj T* (Active Cooler Case for Raspberry Pi 5) Tj T* () Tj T* (Page 13 of 16) Tj T* ET
endstream
